
Your period can bring changes in appetite, energy, mood, and daily comfort. During these days, eating regular, balanced meals can help you stay nourished and keep your routine going. You do not need a complicated diet during menstruation. Simple foods that provide carbohydrates, protein, healthy fats, vitamins, minerals, and enough fluids can be a great choice.

One important nutrient to include in your diet is iron. Since menstruation involves blood loss, iron-rich foods can be useful as part of a balanced eating pattern. Lentils, beans, chickpeas, spinach, peas, tofu, eggs, sesame seeds, and lean meats are examples of foods that can contribute iron. If you eat mostly plant-based foods, combining them with vitamin C-rich foods such as tomatoes, oranges, guava, lemons, or bell peppers can support iron absorption.

Fruits are another easy addition to your period meals. Bananas can be a convenient snack when you want something quick. Oranges and guava provide vitamin C, while watermelon can be refreshing when you want something hydrating. Apples, papaya, grapes, and berries can also be included depending on what is available and what you enjoy.

Whole grains can help provide steady energy. Oats, brown rice, whole-wheat chapati, and other whole-grain foods can be incorporated into breakfast and main meals. You can make oatmeal with banana and nuts, have dal with brown rice, or enjoy chapati with vegetables and a protein-rich curry.

Protein is also useful for making meals satisfying. Eggs, yoghurt, lentils, chickpeas, beans, tofu, paneer, nuts, and seeds can all contribute protein. A simple meal such as dal, rice, and vegetables can provide several nutrients without requiring complicated preparation.

Hydration should not be forgotten. Water is the easiest everyday choice, but soups, coconut water, and water-rich fruits can also contribute to your overall fluid intake. Try keeping a water bottle nearby, especially if you are studying, working, or travelling.

You do not have to completely avoid your favourite foods during your period. If you want chocolate, snacks, or a favourite dessert, you can enjoy them in moderation. A balanced diet is about the overall pattern rather than making every meal perfect.

Along with food, period hygiene is an important part of feeling comfortable. Choose a sanitary pad that suits your flow and personal preferences, and carry a spare when you are away from home. Having your period-care essentials ready can make busy days easier.

